From 315158e3ebbd9abe9ae991a104567025915e2258 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Qianhui Xu <xiaqinghuan@mail.ustc.edu.cn>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 10:26:59 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 3/6] hexagonrpcd: serve DSP writes from a copy-up overlay

The mapped HexagonFS backend is read-only, so a firmware that rewrites its
sensor registry on attach either fails or gets a silent lie.  Add a writable
layer to the HexagonFS instead of special-casing writes in apps_std:

- The overlay is a real directory (-O, e.g. /var/lib/hexagonrpcd) that
  mirrors the DSP-visible paths.  The -R tree stays read-only.
- hexagonfs_openat() serves a file from the overlay when it exists there, so
  the DSP sees what it wrote; everything else still comes from -R, including
  directories and the virtual entries.
- hexagonfs_openat_write() copies the -R file into the overlay first (the
  copy-up), then opens the overlay file read/write.  Files that only exist
  below -R cannot be removed or renamed; the call fails with EPERM.
- File descriptors carry their DSP-visible path (hexagonfs_fd.vpath) and the
  backends allocate them zeroed, so the path can be maintained centrally.

apps_std then just forwards fwrite, fsync, fremove, frename and the
open-for-write case to the HexagonFS.  Without -O, opening a file for
writing fails with the same error as before and the write side stays off.

Verified on a TB322FC (SM8750P) with the sensors PD: the DSP writes its
registry for real (~18 KiB cam_registry_dump.txt, ~14 KiB fstempfile,
sns_reg_version) and iio-sensor-proxy then reports a working accelerometer,
ambient light and proximity sensor.

This change was prepared with AI assistance (pi); the author reviewed the code,
reproduced the measurements on the hardware and takes responsibility for it.

Signed-off-by: Qianhui Xu <xiaqinghuan@mail.ustc.edu.cn>
